/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 140 0 R
/T (Passive euthanasia is defined as the act of letting a patient die naturally by not providing medical treatment that sustains life. Passive euthanasia differs from active euthanasia in that the former does not kill a person, but rather allows an illness or condition to run its course, while the latter involves an action meant to hasten death. )
/E (Passive euthanasia is defined as the act of letting a patient die naturally by not providing medical treatment that sustains life. Passive euthanasia differs from active euthanasia in that the former does not kill a person, but rather allows an illness or condition to run its course, while the latter involves an action meant to hasten death. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 140 0 R
/T (Passive euthanasia often arises when a treatment merely prolongs a dying process without prospects for improvement. Examples include disconnecting machines that keep patients breathing, stopping artificial feeding, and not administering CPR \(Do Not Resuscitate orders\). )
/E (Passive euthanasia often arises when a treatment merely prolongs a dying process without prospects for improvement. Examples include disconnecting machines that keep patients breathing, stopping artificial feeding, and not administering CPR \(Do Not Resuscitate orders\). )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
