/T (Instead of euthanasia, the WMA strongly supports palliative care and pain management  as ethical alternatives. It emphasizes that patients at the end of life should receive comprehensive care that focuses on reducing physical pain, psychological distress, and emotional suffering. The organization argues that improving access to high-quality palliative care reduces the demand for euthanasia by addressing the root causes of suffering. )
/E (Instead of euthanasia, the WMA strongly supports palliative care and pain management  as ethical alternatives. It emphasizes that patients at the end of life should receive comprehensive care that focuses on reducing physical pain, psychological distress, and emotional suffering. The organization argues that improving access to high-quality palliative care reduces the demand for euthanasia by addressing the root causes of suffering. )
